Let  $O$ ,  $H$  and  $R$  be the circumcenter, orthocenter and circumradius of  $\Delta ABC$ . Let  $BC=a, CA=B$  and  $AB=c$ . Then by Law of Sines,   $$ R=\frac{a}{2\sin A}=\frac{a}{2\sin 120^{\circ}}=\frac{a}{\sqrt{3}}. $$  Now, by Law of Cosines, 
\begin{align*}
a^2=b^2+c^2-2bc\cos A 
& = b^2+c^2-2bc\cos 120^{\circ} 
& = b^2+c^2+bc. 
\end{align*}
Finally,
\begin{align*}
OH^2 = 9R^2-a^2-b^2-c^2 
& = 2a^2-b^2-c^2 
& = b^2+c^2+2bc 
& = (b+c)^2. 
\end{align*}  $$ \therefore \boxed{OH=b+c} $$   $QED.$

