Let  $A$  be the green square on top and  $B$  be the pink square on right. By assumption, the blue square does not contain any rooks. Now  $A$  is a  $(2n+1) \times (n-1)$  rectangle. It must contain at most  $2n-2$  rooks (otherwise, some three rooks in same row). So there cannot be a rook in each of the  $2n+1$  rows. Particularly, there is some row  $\mathcal R$  not containing any rooks. Similarly, looking at  $B$  gives there is some column  $\mathcal C$  not containing any rooks. But then the unit square  $\mathcal R \cap \mathcal C$  is not attacked by any rook, contradiction.  $\blacksquare$

