<blockquote>
Let  $ABCD$  be a rectangle and  $P$  be any point on  $\odot(ABCD)$ . Let  $M,K$  be the midpoint of  $CD,AB$  respectively. Let the line perpendicular to  $PK$  at  $K$  intersect  $AD$  at  $Q$ . Then prove that  $QM \parallel PB$ .
</blockquote>
For this let  $PQ \cap \odot(ABCD)=T$  . Note that  $TO \cap PK=Y \in \odot(ABCD) \implies MTKY$  is a parallelogram  $\implies MT \equiv MY \parallel PK \implies \angle PTM=90^\circ \implies TMAQ$  cyclic. Now let  $TP \cap AB=X$ . Note that  $\angle XPB=\angle TAB \equiv \angle TAM=\angle TQM \implies MQ \parallel PB$ . Done  $\blacksquare$ .

