If we let  $x = t$  be an axis of symmetry of  $f(x) = x^4+x^3+ax$  that is parallel to the  $y-$ axis, then we require  $f(t -k)=f(t+k)$  for any  $k \in \mathbb{R}$ : $(t-k)^4+(t-k)^3+a(t-k) = (t+k)^4 + (t+k)^3+a(t+k)$ ;

or  $(t^4 - 4t^3k +6t^2k^2 - 4tk^3 + k^3)+(t^3-3t^2k+3tk^2-k^3)+a(t-k) = (t^4 + 4t^3k +6t^2k^2 + 4tk^3 + k^3)+(t^3+3t^2k+3tk^2+k^3)+a(t+k)$ ;

or  $2ak = -8t^3k-8tk^3-6t^2k-2k^3$ ;

or  $a = -4t^3 - 4tk^2 -3t^2 -k^2 = -k^2(1 + 4t) - (3t^2 + 4t^3)$  (i).

Since  $f(t -k)=f(t+k)$  must be valid for all real  $k$ , we require  $t = -\frac{1}{4}$  in order to make the  $k^{2}-$ term vanish in (i).  This allows to compute  $a = -[3(-1/4)^2 + 4(-1/4)^3] =$   $\fbox{-1/8}$ .

Given the function  $y(x) = \cos 8x + 3\cos 4x +3\cos 2x + 2\cos x$ .
Then  $y'(x)=-8\sin 8x-12\sin 4x-6\sin 2x-2\sin x$ .

Extrema if  $y'(x)=0$ , if  $4\sin 8x+6\sin 4x+3\sin 2x+\sin x=0$ ,
if  $(4\sin 8x+4\sin 4x)+(2\sin 4x+2\sin 2x)+(\sin 2x+\sin x)=0$ ,
if  $4(2\sin 6x\cos 2x)+2(2\sin 3x\cos x)+2\sin \frac{3x}{2}\cos \frac{x}{2}=0$ ,
if  $4(8\sin \frac{3x}{2}\cos \frac{3x}{2}\cos 3x\cos 2x)+2(4\sin \frac{3x}{2}\cos \frac{3x}{2}\cos x)+2\sin \frac{3x}{2}\cos \frac{x}{2}=0$ . $\sin \frac{3x}{2}=0$  with solutions  $x=k\cdot 4\pi$  and  $x=\frac{2\pi}{3}+k \cdot 4\pi$ . $y(\frac{2\pi}{3})=-\frac{9}{2}$ .

